steps.body:
Some functions do a job instead of computing a value. Give them the return type `void`: they print or change something and hand nothing back.

```cpp
void greet(std::string name) {
    std::cout << "Hi, " << name << '\n';
}
```

A void function ends at its closing brace, but a bare `return;` leaves early. That makes a clean guard at the top: check for a bad case, bail out, and keep the main work unindented.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

void greet(std::string name) {
    {{code}}
    std::cout << "Hi, " << name << '\n';
}

int main() {
    greet("Ada");
    greet("");
    greet("Linus");
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
if (name.empty()) {
    return;              // nothing printed
}
```

A **default argument** fills in a parameter the caller leaves out: `void printBanner(std::string title, char border = '*')`. Now `printBanner("Menu")` uses `'*'` and `printBanner("Menu", '=')` overrides it.

Rules and gotchas:

- Defaults go at the end of the list. `void f(int a = 1, int b)` does not compile, because arguments fill parameters left to right.
- You cannot skip a middle argument. With two defaults, setting the second means passing the first too.
- A `std::string` parameter is a full copy of the text. That is fine for now, and the References lesson shows how to avoid it.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

void printBanner(std::string title, char border = '*') {
    if (title.empty()) {
        return;
    }
    const std::string line(title.size(), border);
    std::cout << line << '\n';
    std::cout << title << '\n';
    std::cout << line << '\n';
}

int main() {
    printBanner("Menu");
    printBanner("Settings", '=');
    printBanner("");
    printBanner("End", '-');
    return 0;
}

